<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Static Depth Problem</div>
<h2>Every Input Uses All 32 Layers. That's Rarely Optimal.</h2>
<p>Modern LLMs are designed to run every layer, in fixed order, for every input — whether the question is "What do plants need to grow?" or a multi-step calculus proof. This static architecture is simultaneously wasteful for simple inputs and potentially suboptimal for hard ones: some layers may introduce noise or irrelevant transformations, while others could benefit from being revisited.</p>
<p>The evidence is stark. On LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct, the default 32-layer forward pass achieves only <strong>6% accuracy on DART-2</strong> (a medium-difficulty math benchmark). Yet a customized layer sequence — skipping 6 layers, looping 4 others — raises this to <strong>66.2%</strong>, a >10× improvement, with no weight updates whatsoever.</p>
<p>This is the central claim of <em>Chain-of-Layers (CoLa)</em>: pretrained transformer layers are compositionally generalizable modules, and the right composition for each input is rarely the default one.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">layer-path-contrast.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">fixed depth vs. CoLa-optimized path</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-problem" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canvas above shows two execution paths for the same LLaMA-3-8B model answering a DART-4 math question. The <span style="color:var(--muted)">gray path (left)</span> runs all 32 layers — the standard forward pass, 12% accuracy on DART-4. The <span style="color:#8b5cf6">purple path (right)</span> is CoLa's optimized sequence: fewer unique layers, some repeated — 49.4% accuracy.</p>

<h3>Why Does This Happen?</h3>
<p>Residual connections in transformers create layer similarity — adjacent layers often encode highly correlated representations. For easy inputs, this means many layers are "redoing" work that earlier layers already handled. For hard inputs, a single pass through layer 18 may not be enough; a second pass through the same layer, applied iteratively like an RNN cell, can refine the representation further.</p>
<p>Existing approaches address half the problem: <em>early-exit</em> networks allow shallow inference but only by removing trailing layers; <em>looped transformers</em> allow recurrence but must be trained from scratch for it. CoLa unifies both at test time, without any retraining.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-space" data-code="space">
<div class="section-no">02 — The CoLa Space</div>
<h2>Two Operations: Skip and Recur</h2>
<p>Formally, given a pretrained model with layers [L₁, L₂, …, L₃₂], CoLa constructs a per-input execution path P = [Lᵢ, Lⱼ, Lₖ, …] where each Lᵢ is one of the original layers. Two operations define the space:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skip</strong>: omit a layer entirely from the path — useful when a layer adds noise or redundancy for this particular input</li>
  <li><strong>Recur</strong>: repeat a layer (or block of layers) r times in succession — like unrolling an RNN cell, applying more computation where it helps</li>
</ul>
<p>No new weights are introduced. Each layer's parameters are frozen. Only the <em>sequence</em> of which layers to call, and how many times, changes per input.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">cola-space.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click mode to see different execution paths for a 12-layer model</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns" id="space-mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-mode="original">Original</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-mode="skip">+Skip-only</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-mode="recur">+Recur-only</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-mode="cola">+CoLa</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-space" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The action space at each MCTS step is compact: skip or repeat a contiguous block of k layers (k ∈ &#123;1,2,3,4&#125;), with a repetition count r ∈ &#123;1,2,3,4&#125;. This keeps the search tractable while covering a rich range of architectures.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">CoLa path definition</div>
  <span class="math-var">P</span> = [<span class="math-var">L<sub>i₁</sub>, L<sub>i₁</sub>, …, L<sub>i₁</sub></span>, <span class="math-var">L<sub>i₂</sub></span>, <span class="math-var">L<sub>i₃</sub>, L<sub>i₃</sub></span>, …]
  <span class="math-comment">  ← L<sub>i₁</sub> repeated r=3 times, L<sub>i₂</sub> used once, L<sub>i₃</sub> looped twice</span><br/>
  where each <span class="math-var">L<sub>iₖ</sub></span> ∈ &#123;L₁, …, L<sub>N</sub>&#125;  <span class="math-comment">← original frozen weights</span>
</div>

<h3>Why Not Just Train a Routing Controller?</h3>
<p>You could imagine learning a routing function that decides which layers to skip or repeat. But that requires task-specific training data and introduces distribution shift between training and inference. CoLa's MCTS approach requires only a correctness signal (right or wrong) at test time — making it applicable to any frozen LLM without access to training data or gradients.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-mcts" data-code="mcts">
<div class="section-no">03 — MCTS: Navigating the Path Space</div>
<h2>Monte Carlo Tree Search as Architecture Search</h2>
<p>The space of possible layer paths is combinatorially enormous. For a 32-layer model with skip-and-repeat operations on blocks of up to 4 layers, the number of distinct paths easily exceeds 10¹⁰. Greedy or beam search get stuck in local optima. CoLa frames path construction as a symbolic game and uses MCTS — the same algorithm behind AlphaGo — to efficiently explore it.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="mcts-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Initialize</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Start from the standard forward path P₀ = [L₁, L₂, …, L₃₂]. This is the root node of the MCTS tree. All layer weights remain frozen.</div>
      <div class="step-code">P₀ = [L1, L2, L3, …, L32]  # baseline path</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Selection</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Traverse the tree by selecting child nodes that maximize the UCB score — balancing exploitation (high reward paths) against exploration (rarely-visited paths) while penalizing excessive path length.</div>
      <div class="step-code">UCB(P) = Q(P)/v(P) − λ·||P||/v(P) + c·√(ln V / v(P))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Expansion</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Generate candidate children from the current node by applying one edit: skip k layers or repeat a k-layer block r times (k, r ∈ &#123;1,2,3,4&#125;). Only unexplored children are expanded.</div>
      <div class="step-code">actions = skip(k) | repeat(k, r)  # k, r in &#123;1,2,3,4&#125;</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Simulation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Execute the candidate path on the input. Assign reward=1 if the prediction is correct, minus a path-length penalty λ·‖P‖ to encourage compact solutions. The model answer serves as the feedback signal.</div>
      <div class="step-code">reward = correct(P, x) − λ · normalize(len(P))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Backpropagation &amp; Return</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Propagate the reward up the tree, updating Q(P) and v(P) for all ancestors. After N simulations, return the Pareto-optimal paths balancing accuracy and depth.</div>
      <div class="step-code">return pareto_optimal(accuracy, depth)  # all found paths</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="mcts-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="mcts-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="mcts-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">UCB score</div>
  UCB(<span class="math-var">P</span>) = <span class="math-var">Q(P)</span> / <span class="math-var">v(P)</span>
    &nbsp;−&nbsp; <span class="math-var">λ</span> · ‖<span class="math-var">P</span>‖ / <span class="math-var">v(P)</span>
    &nbsp;+&nbsp; <span class="math-var">c</span> · √(ln <span class="math-var">V</span> / <span class="math-var">v(P)</span>)
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">Q(P) = cumulative reward · v(P) = visit count · V = total simulations</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">λ scales length penalty · c balances exploration</span>
</div>

<p>The length penalty λ in the UCB score is key: it simultaneously drives MCTS toward <em>shorter, more efficient</em> paths while still rewarding correctness. This is what makes CoLa discover not just accurate paths, but <em>minimally-deep</em> ones.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-ablation" data-code="ablation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Skip vs Loop vs Both</div>
<h2>Combining Both Operations is Critical</h2>
<p>The paper runs a careful ablation comparing three search spaces: skip-only, recurrence-only, and the full CoLa (both). The results reveal a complementary relationship — each operation excels in different regimes, and neither alone suffices for the hardest tasks.</p>
<p>Click a benchmark below to see how all three strategies compare on <strong>LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct</strong>. The numbers are the paper's exact accuracy (%) figures from Table 1.</p>

