<p>The pattern is remarkably consistent: <strong>skip-only</strong> dominates on easy tasks (ARC-E/C) because many layers are genuinely redundant for simple factual retrieval. <strong>Recurrence-only</strong> starts pulling ahead on DART-3 and above — iterative refinement of reasoning layers helps. But only <strong>CoLa</strong> (both operations together) achieves the largest gains on the hardest tasks — getting to 47.8% on DART-5 when recurrence-only reaches just 21.6%.</p>
<p>The skip+recurrence combination enables the model to compress redundant layers <em>and</em> expand high-utility ones in the same path — a flexibility neither operation alone can achieve.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-depth" data-code="depth">
<div class="section-no">05 — Depth-Accuracy Tradeoff</div>
<h2>Better Accuracy, Fewer Layers — Even When Correcting Errors</h2>
<p>A natural concern is that CoLa's accuracy gains come at the cost of depth: maybe MCTS just adds more looping layers, making inference slower. The paper's analysis of actual path lengths reveals the opposite.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">depth-accuracy-scatter.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle dataset — arrow points to "better"</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns" id="scatter-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-ds="DART-4">DART-4</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-ds="DART-5">DART-5</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-scatter" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Each point represents one of the 4 strategies applied to LLaMA-3-8B (base and instruct variants) on the selected benchmark. The clear story: <strong>CoLa occupies the top-right "Better" region</strong> — higher accuracy than recurrence-only at roughly similar depth, and higher accuracy than skip-only at slightly deeper paths.</p>
<p>Even more striking is the W→C (wrong→correct) finding. For inputs where CoLa <em>fixes</em> a wrong original prediction, the CoLa paths are often <em>shorter</em> than C→C paths (originally correct, kept correct). Correcting an error doesn't require more computation — it requires <em>reconfiguration</em>, removing the layers that are contributing noise and looping the ones that carry signal.</p>
<p>This challenges the intuition that "more layers = more reasoning capacity." The original model's fixed path may actively hurt performance by running layers that mislead the computation for a given input type.</p>
</section>

